WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:08.820
Can you tell me what is common between a frying
pan, a rope to hang your

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.170
clothes, an air tight container and a pair
of slippers?

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.930
Apart from the fact that they are all very
useful in everyday life, another

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:22.109
common thing is that they are made up of long
chains of repeating units.

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.720
In
simple words, they are made up of POLYMERS.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.529
What exactly do we mean by polymers?

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:35.650
As the name says, “Poly” in Greek means
“Many” and “Mer” refers to “Units”.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.079
So can we say, many repeating units joined
together?

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:40.079
Yes!

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:42.780
That is what it means.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.399
And these units are all the same.

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:48.840
That means, the
basic unit that forms the polymer will repeat

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.309
itself several times.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:53.820
Do we know
what this single unit is called?

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.820
Yes!

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.309
It is called a MONOMER!

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:59.999
So a monomer
repeats itself many times to form extremely

00:00:59.999 --> 00:01:03.999
long chains which form nothing
but the POLYMER!

00:01:03.999 --> 00:01:08.500
Polymers can be naturally occurring, or synthetic
which means man-made.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.651
Now can you tell me… which among these are
natural and which ones are

00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:14.080
artificial?

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.440
Well, all of them are artificial!

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.440
Yes!

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:23.860
The frying pan has a surface of Teflon, the
rope is made up of Nylon, the container is

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.570
Plastic and the slippers are simply
Processed Rubber!

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:32.131
Among all these examples, we are going to
focus on the rope and the

00:01:32.131 --> 00:01:33.830
container.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.970
We will focus on these two because we need
to understand the

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.960
concept of Synthetic fibres and Plastics.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.910
Wait a second….what do we mean by Synthetic
fibres?

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:48.550
We have always used
cotton, wool, or silk which are naturally

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:49.700
occurring.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.300
Do we know what synthetic
fibres are?

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.690
Of course we do!

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.540
The shiny clothes made up of rayon, the warm
and soft

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.030
clothes made up of acrylic are all what we
normally use.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.840
These are all examples of Synthetic fibres.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.470
As the name suggests, these are fibres made
by man.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:13.100
That means the natural
fibres are chemically processed or maybe we

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.470
can even mix a few reagents to
get these artificial fibres?

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.780
Yes!

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.100
Absolutely!

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.880
That is exactly what we do.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.610
There are several ways of obtaining synthetic
fibres.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.800
In the upcoming videos, let us learn about
the types of Synthetic fibres and

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.830
their characteristics.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.320
Then we will learn about Plastics being the
material of

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.670
choice and the effect of Plastic on our environment!

